			As someone who's spent a decade and a half in the mobile trenches, 'Head of Design Engineering' isn't two buzzwords in a trench coat — it's the only sane response to the fact that your design system will look perfect in Figma and then completely fall apart on a 6.1-inch screen with a 60Hz refresh rate and a mid-range GPU. I've lost count of how many times a designer handed me a spec that works beautifully on iOS but has a one-pixel gap on a specific Android OEM skin because their custom renderer doesn't clip shadows the same way. That gap isn't a bug; it's a fundamental mismatch between the design intent and the platform's actual rendering behavior. Someone has to bridge that gap before it becomes a ticket in a backlog that never gets prioritized.

			Most teams treat this as a 'just make the Android build match the iOS build' problem, which is like saying 'just make the cat bark.' A design engineer who actually understands mobile platforms knows that you can't achieve pixel-perfect parity — you have to design for the constraints of each platform and then communicate those constraints back to the design team. On iOS, you have a single vendor controlling both hardware and software. On Android, you have 847 devices with different screen densities, color gamuts, and thermal throttling profiles. Tested on 847 devices, broken on 848 — that's not a joke, that's my Tuesday.

			What I'd want to see from this role is someone who can look at a design token and know immediately how it'll behave on a Samsung Galaxy A12 running Android 11, because that's the device your users actually have, not the latest Pixel. They need to be comfortable with Flutter's rendering pipeline, React Native's Yoga layout engine, and the fact that every platform update breaks something that worked yesterday. If they can get a custom animation to run at 120fps on a $200 phone without dropping frames, and also explain to the design team why that blur effect needs a fallback on mid-tier devices, then they're worth far more than the title. If they're just going to sit in a room and argue about design tokens while the app craters on older Android versions, then yeah, that's two buzzwords in a trench coat.
